# Atomic Structure: From Dalton to Quantum Numbers

> The atom turned out to be divisible, mostly empty, and ruled by
> quantum laws. This lesson traces the ideas from Dalton's solid
> spheres to Schrödinger's probability clouds.

## 1. The Three Subatomic Particles

[definition]
**Atom** — the smallest particle of an element that retains its
chemical identity. At its centre sits a dense, positive **nucleus**
of protons and neutrons; electrons fill the space around it.
[/definition]

| Particle | Symbol | Charge | Relative mass | Discovered by |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Electron | e⁻ | −1 | ~1/1836 | J. J. Thomson (1897) |
| Proton | p⁺ | +1 | 1 | Goldstein (canal rays, 1886) |
| Neutron | n⁰ | 0 | 1 | J. Chadwick (1932) |

The nucleus holds over 99.9% of the atom's mass in a volume about
10⁻⁵ of the whole atom — matter is mostly empty space.

## 2. How the Atomic Model Evolved

@diagram tree id="atomic-models-evolution" layout="tree" aria="Timeline of atomic models from Dalton to Schrodinger"
  "Atomic model" -> "Dalton 1808: solid sphere"
  "Atomic model" -> "Thomson 1904: plum pudding"
  "Atomic model" -> "Rutherford 1911: nuclear atom"
  "Atomic model" -> "Bohr 1913: fixed orbits"
  "Atomic model" -> "Schrodinger 1926: orbitals"
@/diagram

| Model | Scientist (Year) | Picture of the atom | What it could not explain |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Solid sphere | Dalton (1808) | indivisible billiard ball | subatomic particles |
| Plum pudding | Thomson (1904) | electrons studded in a positive sphere | gold-foil deflections |
| Nuclear | Rutherford (1911) | tiny dense nucleus, electrons outside | why electrons do not spiral inward |
| Planetary | Bohr (1913) | fixed orbits with quantized energies | any atom beyond hydrogen |
| Quantum | Schrödinger (1926) | orbitals — clouds of probability | nothing yet; current model |

[example]
**Rutherford's gold foil experiment (1911):** most α-particles
passed straight through — the atom is mostly empty. A few bent at
large angles, and about 1 in 20,000 bounced back, proving the
positive charge lives in a tiny, dense nucleus.
[/example]

## 3. Quantum Numbers: The Electron's Address

| Number | Symbol | Values | What it fixes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Principal | n | 1, 2, 3, ... | shell — size and energy |
| Azimuthal | l | 0 to n−1 | subshell: 0 = s, 1 = p, 2 = d, 3 = f |
| Magnetic | mₗ | −l to +l | orientation of the orbital |
| Spin | mₛ | +½ or −½ | the electron's spin |

Three rules govern how electrons are arranged:

- **Aufbau principle** — fill the lowest-energy subshell first:
  1s → 2s → 2p → 3s → 3p → 4s → 3d; note that 4s fills before 3d.
- **Pauli exclusion principle** — no two electrons share all four
  quantum numbers; each orbital holds at most two, spins opposed.
- **Hund's rule** — equal-energy orbitals fill singly with parallel
  spins before any pairing starts.
- Capacities: s holds 2, p holds 6, d holds 10, f holds 14.

## 4. Configurations of the First 20 Elements

| Z | El | Configuration | Val | Z | El | Configuration | Val |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | H | 1s¹ | 1 | 11 | Na | [Ne] 3s¹ | 1 |
| 2 | He | 1s² | 2 | 12 | Mg | [Ne] 3s² | 2 |
| 3 | Li | 1s² 2s¹ | 1 | 13 | Al | [Ne] 3s² 3p¹ | 3 |
| 4 | Be | 1s² 2s² | 2 | 14 | Si | [Ne] 3s² 3p² | 4 |
| 5 | B | 1s² 2s² 2p¹ | 3 | 15 | P | [Ne] 3s² 3p³ | 5 |
| 6 | C | 1s² 2s² 2p² | 4 | 16 | S | [Ne] 3s² 3p⁴ | 6 |
| 7 | N | 1s² 2s² 2p³ | 5 | 17 | Cl | [Ne] 3s² 3p⁵ | 7 |
| 8 | O | 1s² 2s² 2p⁴ | 6 | 18 | Ar | [Ne] 3s² 3p⁶ | 8 |
| 9 | F | 1s² 2s² 2p⁵ | 7 | 19 | K | [Ar] 4s¹ | 1 |
| 10 | Ne | 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ | 8 | 20 | Ca | [Ar] 4s² | 2 |

[definition]
**Valence electrons** occupy the outermost shell and do all the
bonding; **core electrons** sit in filled inner shells. Chlorine,
ending 3p⁵, has 7 — one short of the octet that drives its hunger
for one more electron.
[/definition]

## 5. Isotopes, Isobars, Isotones, Ions

| Relationship | Same | Different | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Isotopes | protons (Z) | neutrons | ¹H, ²H, ³H; C-12/C-14; U-235/U-238 |
| Isobars | mass number (A) | protons | ⁴⁰Ar and ⁴⁰Ca |
| Isotones | neutrons | protons | ¹⁴C and ¹⁶O (8 each) |
| Ions | element | electron count | Na⁺ (11 p, 10 e) |

Hydrogen's three isotopes get special names:

| Isotope | Protons | Neutrons | Note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Protium ¹H | 1 | 0 | 99.98% of natural hydrogen |
| Deuterium ²H | 1 | 1 | heavy water D₂O — reactor moderator |
| Tritium ³H | 1 | 2 | radioactive; fusion research |

Cations (electrons lost) are smaller than parent atoms; anions
(electrons gained) are larger.

## 6. Emission Spectra: Why Bohr Mattered

An electron pushed to a higher orbit falls back, emitting a photon
of one exact frequency:

@math label="eq:bohr"
E_n = -\frac{13.6\ \mathrm{eV}}{n^2}, \qquad \Delta E = h\nu
@/math

Because only certain jumps are allowed, hydrogen emits sharp **line
spectra** — a fingerprint unique to each element, readable even in
starlight. Hydrogen's lines bunch into the Lyman (UV), Balmer
(visible), and Paschen (infrared) series.

[important title="Where Bohr breaks down"]
The fixed-orbit model works only for one-electron systems —
hydrogen and ions like He⁺ and Li²⁺. Multi-electron atoms need
Schrödinger's orbital picture.
[/important]

## 7. Quick Revision

| Concept | Memory hook |
| --- | --- |
| Nucleus | 99.9% of the mass in a tiny core |
| Gold foil | mostly empty; 1 in 20,000 rebounds |
| n, l, m, s | shell, subshell, orientation, spin |
| Aufbau | lowest energy first — 4s before 3d |
| Pauli | two per orbital, opposite spins |
| Hund | singles before pairs |
| Isotopes | same Z, different neutrons |
